# How do you think with the clock running?
teaches: ["training/time-management"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How should you split your time across a game? Why do slow games make you better faster? What do you do after a bad loss?"
next: {"title":"Where does every lesson show up in one game?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/29-where-does-every-lesson-show-up-in-one.cast"}

## Warm-up: defend, then calculate
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/ppp2ppp/3b4/8/6nq/1QP5/PP1NBPPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [h3], arrows: [{from: h4, to: h2}, {from: d2, to: f3}]}
      - {fen: r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/2nq4/2p5/8/2NB4/PPP1QPPP/3RR1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Rxd6]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: How do you think with the clock running? Today: where your minutes should go. First, two warm-ups.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: Black threatens queen takes h2, mate.
    draw arrow_0

B: Knight to f3. It guards h2 and hits the queen.
    draw arrow_1

A: Calculate one move more: bishop takes h2, check. If the knight takes back, the queen mates.
    highlight piece_d6

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

    pause 1

    ask "Stop the mate." widget chess line true hints ["Which black pieces back up the queen on h2?","A pawn move."] themes ["defence"] voice a
        right: Pawn to h3 blocks the queen's road to h2 and hits the knight. It's the one move that holds.
        wrong: The queen can't mate alone. How does she reach h2?

B: Two pieces backed the mate: the knight and the bishop on d6. I only looked at the queen.
    highlight piece_g4 piece_d6

A: Second one: calculation.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Black's queen is on the d-file, but my own bishop is in the way.
    highlight piece_d6 piece_d3

A: Checks first. Then follow the line to the end.
    highlight piece_d1

    pause 1

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Move the bishop with check.","Then the rook on d1 sees the queen."] themes ["discovered attack"] voice a teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack"]
        right: Bishop takes h7, check. The king takes it, and your rook takes the queen on d6: a bishop for a queen.
        wrong: Which bishop move gives check, so Black has no time to save the queen?

B: Both needed time. I'd have rushed the first and never found the second.
    highlight piece_d6

    pause 0.8

A: Today: how to tell those moments apart, and where your minutes should go.
    highlight piece_h7

## Where the minutes go
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/3NP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 4, moves: [Nxd4, Qxd4]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp1bpp1/3p1n1p/8/2BQP2B/2N5/PPP2PPP/R4RK1 b - - 1 10, moves: [Nxe4, Nxe4, Bxh4], arrows: [{from: e7, to: h4}]}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp1bpp1/3p3p/8/2BQn2B/2N5/PPP2PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 11, moves: [Bxe7, Qxe7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    counts:
      - e4
sources:
    - id: chabris2003
      title: 'Visualization, pattern recognition, and forward search: effects of playing speed and sight of the position on grandmaster chess errors'
      authors: Christopher F. Chabris, Eliot S. Hearst
      year: 2003
      finding: Checked with a chess engine, the same grandmasters made more blunders, and larger ones, in rapid games than in classical (slow) games.
      doi: 10.1207/s15516709cog2704_3
    - id: moxley2012
      title: The role of intuition and deliberative thinking in experts' superior tactical decision-making
      authors: Jerad H. Moxley, K. Anders Ericsson, Neil Charness, Ralf T. Krampe
      year: 2012
      finding: Both strong and weaker chess players chose better moves at the end of their deliberation than the moves they first thought of.
      doi: 10.1016/j.cognition.2012.03.005

    text cue_move5 "forced" x 80 y 450 font_size 40 hidden true
    text cue_4min "4 min" x 80 y 385 font_size 46 hidden true
    text cue_move11 "critical" x 910 y 450 font_size 40 hidden true
    text cue_2sec "2 sec" x 905 y 385 font_size 46 hidden true
    text lab_open "opening" x 180 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    box box_open "3 min" x 180 y 450 width 160 height 150 font_size 44 hidden true
    text lab_mid "middlegame" x 510 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    box box_mid "9 min" x 510 y 450 width 480 height 150 font_size 44 hidden true
    text lab_end "ending" x 840 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    box box_end "3 min" x 840 y 450 width 160 height 150 font_size 44 hidden true
    text cue_rapid "faster: more blunders" cites ["chabris2003"] x 500 y 290 font_size 46 hidden true
    text cue_think "time: better moves" cites ["moxley2012"] x 500 y 215 font_size 46 hidden true

    card title "Where the minutes go"

A: Now that you've warmed up: the clock. Here's one of your games, fifteen minutes plus ten seconds a move.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: Move four: Black takes your knight on d4.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: I spent four minutes here, checking everything.
    draw cue_4min

A: Only your queen can take back. There's one move to play.
    highlight piece_d1

A: A forced recapture deserves seconds:
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

A: a quick look at checks, captures and threats, then play it.
    text tq_1 "checks" x 905 y 480 font_size 32
    text tq_2 "captures" x 905 y 430 font_size 32
    text tq_3 "threats" x 905 y 380 font_size 32

    pause 1

    erase tq_1 tq_2 tq_3

B: By move ten, I was down to five minutes.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Black takes your pawn on e4.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.4

B: So I took back at once. Knight takes e4.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.4

    draw cue_2sec

A: And his bishop takes yours on h4. You're a pawn down.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.4

    pause 1

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 0.8

A: His capture did two things: it took a pawn,
    highlight piece_e4

A: and it opened the bishop's line to h4.
    draw arrow_0

A: A move that changes two things is a critical moment. That's where your minutes go.
    highlight piece_e4 piece_e7

    erase arrow_0

A: So spend them now. Checks, captures and threats, for both sides.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 1

    ask "Find the best move." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first, and not only the obvious one.","The bishop on h4 is attacked. Can it take something first?"] themes ["in-between move","counting"] voice a teaches ["calculation/in-between-move","calculation/counting"]
        right: Bishop takes e7 first, and the queen takes back. Now the knight on e4 is attacked twice and guarded once: it falls next move.
        wrong: Before you take back on e4, look at what your bishop on h4 can take.

B: A piece for a pawn. And I played the other move in two seconds.
    draw count_e4

A: Backwards: minutes on the forced move, seconds on the critical one.
    draw cue_move5 cue_move11 parallel true

    pause 1

    erase position count_e4 cue_move5 cue_4min cue_move11 cue_2sec

A: As a guide, here's a fifteen-minute budget. Say three minutes for an opening you know,
    draw lab_open box_open

A: about nine for the middlegame, where the critical moments usually come,
    draw lab_mid box_mid

A: and three kept for the ending. The ten seconds a move top you up.
    draw lab_end box_end

B: Why not just play blitz? More games, more practice.
    highlight box_open box_mid box_end

A: More games, but in blitz there's only time for your first guess.
    highlight box_mid

A: Even grandmasters blunder more often, and worse, in fast games than in slow ones.
    draw cue_rapid

A: In another study, both strong and weaker players
    highlight cue_rapid

A: picked better moves after thinking than at first glance.
    draw cue_think

A: A slow game gives the Three Questions time to run. That's how the habit gets trained, not skipped.
    highlight box_open box_mid box_end

## Your turn: forced or critical?
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np4/8/3P4/2P1Bb2/PPQ2PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [gxf3]}
      - {fen: r1b1kb1r/pp3ppp/2np1q2/1N2p3/4P3/8/PPP1BPPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nc7+, Kd8, Nxa8]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/1p2bppp/3pbn2/6B1/8/2NQ4/PPB2PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6, g6]}
      - {fen: 4q1k1/1b2rppp/pp6/8/8/8/PP1Q1PPP/2B1R1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Rxe1+, Qxe1, Qxe1#], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    text score_big "Day 28 score" x 500 y 400 font_size 100 hidden true
    text loss_1 "stop" x 500 y 500 font_size 60 hidden true
    text loss_2 "note the move" x 500 y 400 font_size 60 hidden true
    text loss_3 "look tomorrow" x 500 y 300 font_size 60 hidden true
    text syn_1 "forced: seconds" x 500 y 450 font_size 64 hidden true
    text syn_2 "changed: minutes" x 500 y 340 font_size 64 hidden true
    text newgame "new game?" x 500 y 400 font_size 110 hidden true
    mark newgame_x around newgame kind strike hidden true

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you know where the minutes go. Your turn: four positions, scored.
    draw position

    pause 0.8

A: For each one, ask: is the move forced, or did something change? Then think, and run the Three Questions before you play.
    highlight piece_f3

A: First one: Black has just taken your knight on f3.
    point at.ref piece_f3 gesture circle

    pause 1

    ask "What do you play?" widget chess line true hints ["Any check or capture that wins more?","Which piece can take back?"] themes ["counting"] voice a test day28 teaches ["calculation/counting"]
        right: Forced: a move for seconds. Pawn takes f3: no check or capture beats taking back.
        wrong: Run the Three Questions: is there anything better than taking back?

    text tag_1 "forced" x 75 y 400 font_size 36

    pause 1

    erase tag_1

A: Next. Black's queen has just left d8.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 1

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["What did d8 guard?","A knight check."] themes ["fork"] voice a test day28 teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Critical: his queen's move left c7 unguarded. Knight to c7, check, forks the king and the rook. The king steps aside, and the knight takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: The queen used to guard c7. What can land there now?

    text tag_2 "critical" x 75 y 400 font_size 36

    pause 1

    erase tag_2

A: Next: a capture. Follow it to the end.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 1

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen and bishop aim at h7.","Which black piece guards h7?"] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a test day28 teaches ["tactics/removing-the-defender"]
        right: Critical: a capture that changes two things. Bishop takes f6, the knight that guarded h7. Take back, and queen takes h7 is mate, so Black blocks with g6, and you're a knight up.
        wrong: Which black piece stops queen takes h7? Remove it.

    text tag_3 "critical" x 75 y 400 font_size 36

    pause 1

    erase tag_3

A: Last one. You're Black, so the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: White's rook on e1 is attacked twice and guarded once.
    highlight piece_e1 piece_e7 piece_e8

    pause 1

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Count to the end of the trades on e1.","Rook first, queen last."] themes ["back rank","counting"] voice a test day28 teaches ["safety/back-rank","calculation/counting"]
        right: Critical: the count ends in mate. Rook takes e1, check. The queen has to take back, and your queen takes e1: mate on the back rank.
        wrong: Two against two on e1. Who makes the last capture, and is it check?

    text tag_4 "critical" x 75 y 400 font_size 36

    pause 1

    erase tag_4

    erase position

    draw score_big

A: You scored {day28.score} of {day28.total}.
    results test day28

    pause 1

    erase score_big

B: And when I lose a game badly? I click 'new game' straight away.
    draw newgame

A: Then you play the next one angry, and fast: first guesses again.
    draw newgame_x

    erase newgame newgame_x

A: After a bad loss, stop.
    draw loss_1

A: Write down the move where it turned.
    draw loss_2

A: Then look at it tomorrow, with a clear head.
    draw loss_3

    pause 1

A: So the clock isn't the enemy.
    highlight loss_1 loss_2 loss_3

    erase loss_1 loss_2 loss_3

A: Seconds where the move is forced,
    draw syn_1

A: minutes where something changed.
    draw syn_2

    pause 1

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    erase syn_1 syn_2

## Where next
end_page: true

